{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790409435,"nonce":"CHk9/iKdMtZaDMKn7OD7lCtYPMM=","title":"Runtime seam: a host may reserve room past a class instance's fields","message":"An embedder that composes Haxe with other languages (caribou) keeps per-object state for objects other languages hold: which object of another language stands for this one. For objects with no word of their own it keeps that in an address-keyed map behind a lock, taken on every crossing and in the sweep. A host that owns the heap can instead reserve a word past each instance's fields, if instances are allocated through a slot of their own.\n\nSeam: gc_alloc_object(size) -\u003e *mut u8, defaulting to gc_alloc, used by hlp_alloc_obj and hlp_alloc_obj_sized, the two places ash allocates a class instance. RT_VERSION 2 -\u003e 3. With nothing installed nothing changes. Caribou fills it with gc_alloc(size rounded to a word, plus one).","files":null}]}